In TypeScript, there is no built-in way to explicitly state that you know a variable has only one possible type even though its declared type allows for multiple options. However, there are some coding patterns and techniques you could use to accomplish this type of certainty:

1. Narrowing the type using conditional types: If you're confident that only one value satisfies certain conditions, then you can use conditional types such as `Extract<T, U>` or `NonNullable<T>`. Here's an example:

   ```typescript
   interface A { x: number | string; }
   interface B { x: boolean; y: number; }

   const obj: A = { x: true }; // still has type A with x being a string or number
   if (typeof obj.x === 'boolean') {
     const narrowedObj: NonNullable<A> & B = obj; // x is known to be boolean here
   }
   ```

2. Using type guards: You can also write custom functions that check the value of a property and return a new type based on the results. These functions can then be passed to the `as` keyword to restrict the variable's type. Example of using a custom function as type guard:

   ```typescript
   interface A { x: number | string; }
   interface B { x: boolean | string; }
   interface C extends A { x: boolean; }

   function getType(value: A | B): 'A' | 'B' | 'C' {
     if ((value instanceof Object) && (value.hasOwnProperty('x')) && (typeof value.x === 'boolean')) {
       return 'C';
     } else if (typeof value.x === 'number') {
       return 'A';
     } else {
       return 'B';
     }
   }

   const objA: A = { x: 1 };
   const objB: B = { x: 'hello' };
   const objC: C = { x: true };

   const narrow1: C = objC as C;
   const narrow2: never = objA as C;
   const narrow3: never = objB as C;
   ```

These approaches enable you to have a higher degree of confidence when working with variables and limit any unwanted bugs at the same time. But as you already expected, they come with their limitations. For instance, type narrowing adds more code complexity, makes debugging harder, and violates the "type now, refactor later" principle. While type guards bring even more complexity with a higher cognitive load. In conclusion, neither approach offers a perfect solution to your problem. But using them in appropriate circumstances where you feel confident about your data, might make a difference between code quality and a bad experience for developers trying to understand and maintain your codebase afterward.

